 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "oxford_lhuyd",
   "title": "Edward Lhuyd",
   "text": "Edward Lhuyd, a Welshman who ran the Ashmolean Museum, travelled through Wales, Cornwall, Brittany, Ireland and Scotland collecting words. In 1707, in his Archaeologia Britannica, he showed that Welsh, Cornish, Breton, Irish and Scottish Gaelic were related: the family we now call the Celtic languages.",
   "people": "celts"
  },
  {
   "x": 69,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "COWLEY ROAD: closed for resurfacing. Again. (The shark is that way, all the same.)"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "oxford_carfax",
   "title": "Carfax",
   "text": "Carfax is the crossroads at the centre of Oxford, and its name comes from the Latin quadrifurcus, 'four-forked', for the four roads that meet here. The tower is all that is left of St Martin's church, and climbing its 99 steps gets you the best view over the spires. Its clock has two figures, called quarterboys, that strike the bells on the quarter hour."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "oxford_radcliffe",
   "title": "The Radcliffe Camera",
   "text": "Built between 1737 and 1749 with money left by John Radcliffe, the royal physician, and designed by James Gibbs. 'Camera' is Latin for a room, and this round, domed one has been a Bodleian reading room since 1860. Its rotunda is one of the most photographed buildings in England, and it is full of people being told to be quiet."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "oxford_bodleian",
   "title": "The Bodleian Oath",
   "text": "The Bodleian Library, founded in 1602 by Sir Thomas Bodley, receives a copy of every book published in the UK. To read here you must make a declaration, and it is a famous one: not to remove, mark or damage any book, 'nor to bring into the Library or kindle therein any fire or flame, nor to smoke in the Library'. Since 1602, nobody has been caught kindling a fire, or at least nobody will admit it."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "oxford_bridge_of_sighs",
   "title": "The Bridge of Sighs",
   "text": "Hertford Bridge was built in 1914 to link the old and new parts of Hertford College across New College Lane. It is nicknamed the Bridge of Sighs after the one in Venice, though it looks more like the Rialto, and nobody has been led over it to prison, only to a tutorial. Students say the sighing was at the exam results."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "oxford_tom_tower",
   "title": "Great Tom",
   "text": "Tom Tower at Christ Church holds Great Tom, a bell weighing over six tonnes. Every night at 9.05 pm it rings 101 times, once for each of the original students of the college, to tell them to be in. Oxford lies about five minutes west of Greenwich, so 9.05 by the clock was nine o'clock by Oxford's own time."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "oxford_alice",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Alice and the Dean's Daughter",
   "text": "Alice Liddell was the daughter of Henry Liddell, Dean of Christ Church, and in 1862 the college mathematics tutor Charles Dodgson, who wrote as Lewis Carroll, rowed her and her sisters up the river and told the story that became Alice's Adventures in Wonderland. The sweet shop across the road from the college is said to be the one in the books, and the Dodo in the story is Dodgson himself, who stammered his own name as 'Do-Do-Dodgson'."
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "oxford_magdalen",
   "title": "May Morning",
   "text": "On 1 May at six in the morning the choir of Magdalen College sings a Latin hymn from the top of the tower, and crowds gather on the bridge and in the street below to hear it, then dance morris and drink at breakfast. Magdalen, by the way, is said 'Maudlin', one of Oxford's many traps for visitors."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "oxford_botanic_garden",
   "title": "The Botanic Garden",
   "text": "The oldest botanic garden in Britain, founded in 1621 as a physic garden to grow plants for medicine. It stands behind a high wall beside Magdalen Bridge, and its greenhouses hold everything from Amazon water lilies to carnivorous plants. Oxford gives it a few acres for growing things and a great many students for walking past."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "oxford_castle",
   "title": "Oxford Castle",
   "text": "The Normans built Oxford Castle in 1071, and its tower, St George's, is still standing. In the winter of 1142 the Empress Matilda, besieged here by her cousin Stephen in the war called the Anarchy, escaped across the frozen river in a white cloak to blend in with the snow. Later it was the county prison, until 1996."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "oxford_punting",
   "title": "Punting",
   "text": "Punts are flat-bottomed boats driven by a long pole pushed against the river bed. In Cambridge the punter stands on the raised flat deck at the back, called the till; in Oxford, the proud tradition is to stand at the sloping end instead. Each city says the other has it backwards, and punters in both occasionally fall in."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "oxford_shark",
   "title": "The Headington Shark",
   "text": "In 1986 a seven-and-a-half-metre fibreglass shark was fixed headfirst through the roof of an ordinary terraced house in Headington, east Oxford, on the anniversary of the bombing of Nagasaki, as a protest about nuclear war. The council tried to have it removed; the planning inspector decided it could stay, and it has become one of the best-loved sights in the city."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "oxford_ashmolean",
   "title": "The Ashmolean",
   "text": "The oldest public museum in Britain, opened in 1683, and it began with the collection of the Tradescants, gardeners and travellers, which included a dodo. The dodo rotted in the end and was thrown out, apart from its head and a foot, which went to the Museum of Natural History. Today the Ashmolean's treasures include King Alfred's Jewel, made around 880 AD."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "oxford_inklings",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Inklings",
   "text": "The Eagle and Child, locally the Bird and Baby, is the pub where the Inklings, an informal group of writers, met in the back room from the 1930s to 1962. Two of them, J. R. R. Tolkien and C. S. Lewis, read chapters of their unfinished books aloud, got criticised by their friends and carried on drinking. The pub closed in 2020 and is being restored to open again. The back room is waiting."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "oxford_oed",
   "title": "The Oxford English Dictionary",
   "text": "The OED began in 1857 and was seventy years in the making. Its great editor, James Murray, ran it from a corrugated-iron shed called the Scriptorium in his garden in north Oxford, and thousands of volunteers posted in quotations on paper slips. It records the history of more than 600,000 words, tracing each one back to its first known use."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "oxford_covered_market",
   "title": "The Covered Market",
   "text": "Opened in 1774 to clear the messy street stalls out of the High Street and Cornmarket, and still going. More than fifty units sell fish, game, cheese, flowers, cakes and the odd pie, in a Georgian arcade that smells of coffee."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "oxford_dreaming_spires",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The City of Dreaming Spires",
   "text": "The poet Matthew Arnold called Oxford 'that sweet city with her dreaming spires' in a poem of 1866. The spires, towers and domes are all different, and the skyline from the hills is a tangle of stone. Oxford has been teaching since about 1096, which makes it the oldest university in the English-speaking world."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "oxford_olney_pancake",
   "title": "The Olney Pancake Race",
   "text": "PANCAKE RACE. Every Shrove Tuesday the women of Olney, Buckinghamshire, run 415 yards to the church in aprons and headscarves, tossing a pancake in a frying pan at the start and the finish. By tradition it has been run since 1445. The town of Liberal, Kansas, runs the same race and compares times."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "oxford_poohsticks",
   "title": "Poohsticks",
   "text": "POOHSTICKS. The World Poohsticks Championships began in 1984 at Day's Lock on the Thames, in Oxfordshire, and raise money for charity. The game itself comes from A. A. Milne's Winnie-the-Pooh: drop a stick from the upstream side of a bridge, and the first to come out the other side wins."
  }
